Omaha_World_Herald_1978_10_01_241UmpireWAndy.jpg  Bruce Froemming  page 2  Jocko Conlan, said in his ’67 book, “Jocko:’’ Andy quote
// The press has always found him quotable. Once an Eau Claire manager rushed Froemming on a disputed call and roared, ‘‘You little. . . (expletive deleted).””
In the blink of an eye, Froemming ejected him. Afterward, the umpire told reporters: ‘‘I wouldn’t have given
him the thumb if he’d called me a big so and so, but little? I can’t tolerate that.”’
///
For an umpire, Froemming had said repeatedly, there is only one place to be—the big leagues. Everything else is Poverty Row.
Can a short man make it as a major league umpire? Not many have. Most umpires are of average height or better; some, in fact, stand very tall.
Tallness, society seems to be saying, commands respect and lends itself to authority.
Because of his size, Froemming had a strike against him from the outset maybe two. And he’s no gazelle on the
field. Yet, as the super-ump of yesteryear, Jocko Conlan, said in his ’67 book, “Jocko:’’‘I talked to some fellows
about Froemming. Andy Cohen, the ballplayer, said: ‘He reminds me of you, Jocko. He’s a nice boy, but he’s tough!’””
How can the big leagues deny a fellow like that? Not forever. In 1971 Froemming’s dream came true. The news
came in a long awaited phone call from the National League’s supervisor of umpires.

“Bruce, we're bringing you up,”’ the supervisor said tersely.
Then 31, Froemming had served 13 long, lean years in the minors. He had worked six leagues— Midwest, Nebras-
ka State, Northwest, Northern, Texas and Pacific Coast—and had the lumps to prove it.


